## 1. The objective, quoted in full (idea-repo#5)

> The previous world in this line of work delivered a complete paper, ready to submit to ICLR 2027: "When Do Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures Collapse? Survival as a Race Against Predictor Decay". Its research, code, results and figures are in your repositories and workspaces, and they are finished.
>
> This objective is the writing of that paper, and only the writing. Make it a paper a reviewer would call clearly and convincingly written: the structure and order of the argument, how each claim is stated and supported, the wording, how figures and tables are introduced and interpreted, the captions, and whether each citation sits in a sentence about the cited work. Where the text claims more or less than the evidence in the repositories shows, change the text to match the evidence.
>
> Do not run new experiments, do not change the results or the conclusions, and do not add research directions. A figure or table may be redrawn or laid out again only for readability, from results that already exist. Work that is not the writing answers what the writing asks of it, and otherwise has nothing to do in this objective.
>
> Deliver the revised paper: the LaTeX source in the ICLR 2027 template and the compiled PDF, within the ICLR 2027 submission requirements, the page limit of the main text included, with the appendices kept as supplementary material. Record next to the paper what was changed and why.
>
> Do not use any paper, preprint, code release or other written work first published after 18 October 2026.

## 2. Requirements (numbered "W" for writing, "K" for constraints, "D" for deliverables)

The standard (from #5): a paper **a reviewer would call clearly and
convincingly written**. Each item below quotes the words of #5 it comes from.

**What the writing must achieve**

- **W1. Structure and order of the argument** ("the structure and order of
  the argument"). The sections, their order and what each one establishes
  before the next needs it.
- **W2. Each claim stated and supported** ("how each claim is stated and
  supported"). Every claim is stated once, plainly, with its scope, and next
  to the evidence (theorem, figure, table, test) that supports it.
- **W3. Wording** ("the wording"). Sentences a reader outside the project can
  follow: defined terms, no internal labels, readable number density.
- **W4. Figures and tables introduced and interpreted** ("how figures and
  tables are introduced and interpreted"). Each is referred to in the text
  before or where it is needed, and the text says what to read off it and
  what it shows.
- **W5. Captions** ("the captions"). Each caption says what is plotted and
  what it shows; encodings, n and the statistic are stated.
- **W6. Each citation in a sentence about the cited work** ("whether each
  citation sits in a sentence about the cited work"). A citation is attached
  to a statement of what that work does or shows, and that statement is
  accurate (Section 6 below, and `refs/citation_audit.md` once pushed).
- **W7. Text matches the evidence** ("Where the text claims more or less than
  the evidence in the repositories shows, change the text to match the
  evidence"). Both directions: overclaims weakened, underclaims (results the
  repositories support but the text hides or understates) stated. The
  evidence is the delivered `results/`, `theory/` notes and `code/` checks,
  with `paper/CLAIMS.md` as the existing ledger (Section 5 lists candidates).

**What must not change (constraints)**

- **K1. Writing only** ("the writing of that paper, and only the writing").
- **K2. No new experiments** ("Do not run new experiments"). Recomputing a
  quantity from existing result files for a redrawn figure is not a new
  experiment; new runs, new seeds, new grids or new scoring rules are.
- **K3. Results and conclusions unchanged** ("do not change the results or
  the conclusions"). Every number stays as `numbers.tex` generates it from
  `results/`; every verdict (pass, fail, undetermined, retrospective, post
  hoc) stays. Where W7 requires a wording change, it changes how a result is
  stated, not the result. If the writing finds a result that is wrong, that
  is raised with its owner (experiment-repo), not rewritten.
- **K4. No new research directions** ("do not add research directions").
  Future-work sentences may name what is left open; they add no new study.
- **K5. Figures/tables redrawn or laid out again only for readability, from
  existing results** ("A figure or table may be redrawn or laid out again
  only for readability, from results that already exist"). Owner:
  figtab-repo (assignment: `paper/figures/`, `paper/tables/`,
  `code/figures/`).
- **K6. Other functions only answer the writing** ("Work that is not the
  writing answers what the writing asks of it, and otherwise has nothing to
  do in this objective").
- **K7. Cutoff** ("Do not use any paper, preprint, code release or other
  written work first published after 18 October 2026"): work first
  published on 2026-10-18 or earlier may be used; later may not. This holds
  for any citation added or changed during the rewrite.

**What is delivered**

- **D1. The LaTeX source in the ICLR 2027 template.**
- **D2. The compiled PDF.**
- **D3. Within the ICLR 2027 submission requirements, the page limit of the
  main text included.** The template says: "There will be a strict upper
  limit of **9 pages** for the main text of the initial submission, with
  unlimited additional pages for citations." The AI use statement is
  "**required** and does not count toward the page limit"; the ethics and
  reproducibility statements are "recommended" and do not count. venue-repo
  owns the full rule set and the checker.
- **D4. "with the appendices kept as supplementary material."** Read: the
  appendices stay (they are not cut) and are supplementary, i.e. outside the
  9-page main text, after the references in the same PDF as the template's
  `\appendix` section allows, or as a separate supplementary file;
  venue-repo decides which. **Consequence for the writing (my reading, the
  most important one):** reviewers are not obliged to read supplementary
  material, so the 9-page main text must state and support its claims on its
  own; an appendix may hold proofs, details and further results, but a main
  claim whose only support is an appendix figure or table is not
  "supported" in the sense of W2 (Section 4.4).
- **D5. "Record next to the paper what was changed and why."** A change
  record in `paper/` beside `main.tex` (paper-repo; e.g. a rewritten
  `paper/CHANGES.md` section for #5), listing each change and its reason
  (W1-W7), including every place where W7 changed what a sentence claims
  and the evidence that required it.

## 3. The starting point (released paper, paper-repo 771f5a90e56b)

Title: "When Do Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures Collapse? Survival
as a Race Against Predictor Decay". Main text (pp. 1-9): 1 Introduction,
2 Related work, 3 Setup, 4 Driving force versus decay: the escape race
(4.1 exact structure, 4.2 the race), 5 What the heuristics change,
6 Experiments (6.1 E1b, 6.2 E2 masking, 6.3 E3 ViT), 7 Beyond gradient
descent, encoder depth and the near-collapse window (7.1 AdamW, 7.2
layer-normalised target, 7.3 deep linear encoder, 7.4 weight-decay schedules,
7.5 the race and its premise in the ViT), 8 Limitations and conclusion;
statements; references; App. A Proofs, B Further limits and predictions
(incl. Table 1 heuristics, structure of the aligned case, extended related
work), C-E experimental details (E1-E3), F the extension (derivations, tests,
Table 8 of all extension tests). Floats in the main text: two figures (Fig. 1
