What the paper claims (fixed by K3; the ledger is `paper/CLAIMS.md`, the
status per claim in `paper/CHANGES.md`): C1 exact structure near collapse
(proved; framing credited to De Melo Costa et al. 2026); C2 the race predicts
collapse and rank (held-out, pre-registered, linear GD: E1b MAE 0.25, masking
E2 0.03; not the law under AdamW or a deep encoder; in the ViT predicts the
epoch-50 critical decay but not shown better than the step-0 rule per run);
C3a-d how predictor, masking, target update and weight decay act (linear GD);
C4 setting heuristics from the criterion: not supported; C5 where the theory
fails; N1 AdamW near-collapse dynamics (aligned basis; fixed-horizon count
passes, converged rank fails); N2 layer-normalised target (passes, linear,
GD); N3 schedules (fails as registered); N4 the ladder (rung 1a aligned
passes / generic fails; 1b passes; rung 2 passes; rung 3 fails; rung 4
undetermined); N5 collapse is not decided early in the ViT (premise D <= 5
holds in no cell); N6 race vs the mu count of 2610.02344 (retrospective;
orders their tabular grid worse than their count).

## 4. What a reviewer would find unclear: diagnosis of the released text

My reading of the released PDF and source, given as material for the plan
(story-repo) and the writing (paper-repo); it is not a verdict (review owns
that). Locations are released page/line numbers or `sections/<file>.tex`.

### 4.1 Structure and order (W1)

1. **The paper reads as a paper plus an appended extension.** Sections 3-6
   build and test the race for linear GD; Section 7 then leaves each
   assumption "in turn" with five unrelated subsections (AdamW, LN target,
   deep encoder, schedules, ViT premise), and Section 8 is one paragraph. The
   introduction's three contribution bullets mirror this history (theory,
   tests, "beyond"). There is no single line of argument a reader can state
   after the introduction (e.g. "where the race holds, where it is replaced,
   where its premise fails"), and the ViT material is split between 6.3 (E3,
   step-0 rule) and 7.5 (race readout and switch experiment).
2. **The central premise is tested last and fails there.** The paper is
   built on "collapse is decided early" (Sec. 1 p. 1 l. 46) and Section 7.5
   ends "the premise fails" in the ViT. The order in which the reader meets
   the premise, its support (linear models) and its failure (ViT) is a
   structural choice the plan must make explicitly; the title and abstract
   must stay consistent with it (Section 5).
3. **No discussion section.** What the results mean together (which
   heuristic acts through which term, what a practitioner can and cannot
   take from them, given C4 not supported and N3 recommending no schedule)
   is spread over limitations clauses.
4. **Related work comes before the setup it is positioned against** and
   positions by equation and section numbers the reader has not seen yet
   (Sec. 2 cites Definition 4, (13), Section 5, Section 7.1, 7.3).

### 4.2 Claims stated and supported (W2)

5. **Abstract and contributions are lists of numbers.** The abstract has
   about 15 numbers with baselines in parentheses and 8 results; the second
   and third contribution bullets repeat them. A reader cannot tell the main
   claim from the secondary ones.
6. **Claims carry their test history in the sentence** ("A first frozen
   model (v3) predicted … with (iv) added (v4) a new grid passes", Sec. 7.1;
   "known before the last scorer fix", Sec. 7.5; "the word is undetermined
   (truth ill-posed)", Sec. 7.3). The provenance belongs in the appendix
   and the change record; the main text needs the claim, its scope and its
   verdict.
7. **Scope qualifiers are scattered.** Which results are linear-only, GD-only,
   aligned-basis-only, retrospective or post hoc is said in many places
   with different words; the reader needs one consistent convention (e.g.
   "registered held-out test", "retrospective", "post hoc") defined once.

### 4.3 Wording (W3)

8. **Internal labels in the main text** (counts in main-section sources):
   "rung" (4), "Arm T/P" (4), E1b/E1c/E2/E3 (17), A1 (5), N2 (3), v3/v4 (3),
   B1′, B3, "set c", "A2′ sets", "Th", "TOST", "the word", "mu_tight". These
   are names of the project's work packages and registrations; a reviewer
   has no use for them unless each is defined, and most need not be.
9. **Density.** Parenthetical baseline lists with intervals in most
   sentences of Sections 6-7 (e.g. Sec. 7.2 lists seven errors in two
   sentences). The numbers stay (K3); the main text can give the comparison
   that carries the claim and send the rest to a table.
10. **Inconsistent cross-reference names:** appendices are printed as
    "Section A", "Section D" by `\cref` and as "App. B" by hand in the same
    paragraph (Sec. 5, p. 6); ICLR papers conventionally say "Appendix A".

### 4.4 Figures and tables (W4, W5, D4)

11. **The main text leans on appendix floats.** It cites Figure 3b (Sec.
    4.2, the drive-vs-decay schematic, in App. A), Table 1 (heuristics, App.
    B), Tables 4, 5, 7 (E1b/E2/E3 criteria, App. E), Figures 7 and 9 (E2,
    E3, App. E), Figure 13 and Table 8 (App. F). Under D4 the claims these
    support need their evidence in the 9 pages or a sentence that carries
    it. Only two figures and no table are in the main text.
12. **Figure 2 is a composite of three unrelated results** (AdamW count vs
    weight decay; deep-encoder model vs reading; ViT switch share f(e)),
    referenced from three subsections, with a caption that is a legend
    (colour and marker list) without the finding.
13. **Figure 1's caption** gives encodings but not what the reader should
    see (the rescue-then-removal in lambda and the boundary far below the
    static threshold are said in the text, not the caption).
14. **Figure numbering vs. order**: main-text Figures 1-2, then the reader
    is sent to Figure 3 (appendix schematic) from Section 4 before Figure 1.
    K5 allows moving or re-laying out figures for readability (figtab-repo).

### 4.5 Citations (W6)

15. **Citation audit (all 109 citation commands, 54 keys; `refs/citation_audit.md`,
    each verdict with its source):** 98 OK, 7 vague, 4 misattributed. The
    four misattributions, which a reviewer would object to most (two of
    them in the main text):
    - `related.tex` l. 14 (Sec. 2): De Melo Costa et al. are said to read
      EMA as a shift of their ratio; they treat EMA as a timescale that does
      not decide which modes escape (their "Mechanism 3: EMA as a
      timescale", Prop. J.7). The paper's own `related_ext.tex` l. 31 says
      it correctly, so the main text contradicts the appendix. (Brief v4
      and v5.0 repeated the error; corrected in Section 6 below.)
    - `related.tex` l. 11 (Sec. 2) and `related_ext.tex` l. 16: Ponce et
      al. (2025) are credited with "an optimal ridge predictor" and the rule
      c^2 >= 4 lambda; they train the predictor (ridge form only at
      equilibria, their Eq. 7) and give the threshold for scalar input
      (Prop. 4.11); and they do not leave EMA stability open (Prop. 4.10).
      All of it is true of Arbel et al. (PEIRA, 2026).
    - `related_ext.tex` l. 60: Li, Luo & Lyu (2021) have no weight decay;
      "with weight decay a mode grows only if its signal exceeds the
      decay" is our extension.
    Vague: Xie & Li (2024) KKT point applied to a non-gradient field
    (`extension.tex` l. 20); Kunstner et al. 2024 / Sabanayagam & Lucey
    2026 as a tag on our sign-step approximation (l. 19); Bao 2023
    (`related_ext.tex` l. 19) and Chen et al. 2023 (`discussion.tex` l. 6)
    without what they show; the introduction's "Several have partial
    theory (...)" list. The audit gives a minimal rewording for each.
    Checked and correct: Tian et al.'s eqs. 11-12, 14 and Theorem 1 (the
    per-mode match is exact at alpha = 1), Jacobs et al.'s Lemma 4.5, the
    De Melo Costa section/equation pointers. Bibliography: all 54 entries
    cited, all first published on or before 2026-10-18 (latest:
    Sabanayagam & Lucey 2026-10-03, De Melo Costa 2026-10-01); three
    entries give the arXiv version where a published one exists by the
    cutoff (`ponce2025dual` ICLR 2026, `bisulco2025masked` ICCV 2025,
    `bao2023feature` Neural Computation).

## 5. Where the text may claim more or less than the evidence (W7): candidates

Candidates found while reading; each is to be checked against the results
by the writer and review, not taken as established. None changes a result.

1. **Introduction, p. 1 l. 44-45:** "no criterion predicts when a
   configuration collapses and how much rank it keeps", followed three lines
   later by De Melo Costa et al. (2026), whose stability count bounds the
   rank (and on their own tabular grid orders it better than the race, N6).
   Overclaim about the literature; the gap needs stating as what that count
   lacks (predictor decay, the race in time), as Section 2 already does.
2. **Section 7 opening:** "Every prediction was frozen before its held-out
   runs and scored once" sits beside a post hoc post-loss law (B1′), a
   retrospective rung-2 score on E2's arm, a post hoc tanh block-mask
   result, and a ViT ratio "known before the last scorer fix" (CHANGES.md
   row C2). The sentence is broader than the record; the exceptions are
   stated later, but the general sentence should not exceed them.
3. **Limitations, p. 9 l. 483-484:** "5 seeds per row, hardware confounded
   with row". E3's registered rows have 3 seeds (Sec. 6.3; App. E
   `sections/appendix.tex` l. 244, "3 seeds (0--2) per configuration"), the
   E7/E8 rows 5; and the E7-G rerun (`results/e7/e7g.json`; CHANGES.md row C2)
   found no GPU-family difference on the reference row at 3 seeds. The
   sentence should give the seeds per experiment and say what the confound
   is and what E7-G does and does not exclude.
4. **Abstract, last sentence:** "switching weight decay mid-training shows
   that collapse is not decided early" generalises from one small ViT on
   CIFAR-10; the evidence supports "in this ViT" and, for lowering decay,
   "partly in one arm, not at all in the other" (as written).
5. **Contribution 1** lists "how predictor learning rate, depth and target
   speed act" among the race's predictions; the depth prediction failed and
   was corrected post hoc (E1c), one EMA sign failed (13/14), and in the ViT
   predictor and EMA predictions failed (Table 7). The bullet states the
   predictions without their record; the scope must be visible where the
   claim is made.
6. **Underclaims:** the E1b collapse-boundary result (λc 0.023-0.088
   against static 0.375, 1 of 72 cells missed) and the N2 result (MAE 0.045,
   every run within one) are among the strongest evidence and are buried in
   long sentences; the rung-2 pass (the race extends to a two-layer MLP
   encoder when it reads the decaying first layer's features) is given one
   clause in Sec. 7.3.
7. **Section 2, p. 2 l. 93:** "We recover these results as limits (Section
   D)". Section D (extended related work, `sections/related_ext.tex` l. 8-25,
   58-64) describes those works but derives none of them as a limit. Two
   are recovered in the main text: the static threshold of Tian, Wang, Ponce
   and Arbel as Prop. 2(b), the balanced basin of Tian et al. as Cor. 3.
   Mode-by-mode learning from small initialisation (Saxe; Simon; Li) is not
   derived anywhere I found. The sentence should name what is recovered and
   where (checked 2026-10-22).
8. **Section 6.3:** "Spearman 0.83 [0.45, 0.99], carried by the weight-decay
   sweep": CLAIMS.md records the post hoc split (1.0 over the six
   weight-decay configurations, 0.27 [-0.58, 1.0] over the other six); the
   main text's "carried by" should say this is post hoc.

## 6. Prior work and citation conventions

- The prior-work map of brief v4.3 stands for the cited works (it is in
  `refs/brief_v4_objective3.md`, Sections 5 and A5, with equation-level notes
  on arXiv 2610.02344 in `refs/lit_dvd2610.md`). #5 adds no research
  direction, so no new body of prior work is needed; a citation may be
  added only where a sentence about existing content needs its source, and
  then only work first published on or before 2026-10-18 (K7).
- Positioning to keep (from v4.2-4.3, already in the paper): De Melo Costa
  et al. 2026 (arXiv 2610.02344, NeurIPS 2026) is prior work; it is credited
  with the drive-vs-decay framing, the rank as a count of unstable modes, and
  reading predictor scale, masking and encoder weight decay as shifts of its
  ratio, and EMA as a timescale that does not decide which modes escape
  (their "Mechanism 3: EMA as a timescale", Prop. J.7);
  its escape condition with encoder weight decay coincides with
  the paper's initial window in the aligned model; it has no predictor weight
  decay. What stays new: the decaying gain, collapse as an attractor, the
  race and lambda^race, the need for predictor decay, the held-out tests.
  Tian et al. 2021 is credited with the per-mode system, the conserved
  quantity (their Theorem 1) and the static threshold; Prop. 1-2 and Cor. 3
  are "known in substance".
- Convention for W6 (what a reviewer checks): each citation sits in a clause
  that says what the cited work does ("X show that ..."; "as in X, we ...");
  a group citation is used only for a statement true of each member; a
  theorem/equation number attributed to a paper is that paper's own.
- Reference list: 54 entries in `paper/references.bib`; every arXiv id and
  v1 date was verified in v3-v4 (verified list in
  `refs/brief_v4_objective3.md`, Appendix B); the date check of every entry
  against K7 is part of `refs/citation_audit.md`.

## 7. Conventions for a clearly written ICLR theory-and-experiments paper

Field conventions the writing can follow (general practice, not rules of
#5; the venue rules are venue-repo's):
- Introduction ends with a short list of contributions, each a claim with
  its scope and a pointer to where it is supported; numbers only for the
  headline result.
- Definitions, assumptions and results as numbered environments; each
  approximation labelled as such with where its accuracy is measured (the
  paper already does this; keep it).
- Experiments sections say, before the results, what is tested, against
  which baselines and how scored (pre-registered held-out vs development vs
  post hoc); results paragraphs open with the finding.
- Every main-text figure/table is referred to in the text, in order, and
  interpreted there; captions are self-contained: what is shown, the
  finding, n, statistic and interval type.
- Appendices are named "Appendix X" and referenced for proofs and details,
  not for the evidence of a main claim (D4).
- Limitations and failed predictions stated plainly in the main text (the
  paper's honesty about failed tests is a strength to keep, not to hide).

